  <div id="calc-macros-explain" class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">What Are Macros & Why They Matter</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Macronutrients — <strong>protein</strong>, <strong>carbohydrates</strong>, and <strong>fat</strong> — are
        the three nutrients that supply your body with energy (unlike micronutrients like vitamins and
        minerals, which don't provide calories). Protein and carbohydrate each provide about 4 calories per
        gram, while fat provides about 9 — more than double — which is why fat-heavy foods pack more calories
        into a smaller volume.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Protein</strong> supplies the amino acids your body uses to repair and build muscle tissue, and
        is the macro most linked to preserving lean mass while eating in a calorie deficit. It's also the most
        satiating macro gram-for-gram and has the highest thermic effect of food, meaning your body burns more
        calories digesting it than it does digesting carbs or fat.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Carbohydrates</strong> are your body's preferred fuel for the brain and for higher-intensity
        exercise, and getting enough supports training performance and recovery. Whole-food sources — vegetables,
        fruit, whole grains, legumes — also supply fiber, which supports digestion and satiety.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Fat</strong> is required for hormone production (including sex hormones), supports cell membrane
        structure, and is necessary to absorb the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K. Going too low on fat for
        extended periods can disrupt hormone levels.
        <br /><br />
        Total calories are still what determines whether you lose, maintain, or gain weight — a macro split
        doesn't override that. What it changes is body composition, hunger, energy levels, and how sustainable a
        calorie target feels day to day, which is why picking a split you can actually stick with matters as
        much as the exact percentages. For context, the Institute of Medicine's Acceptable Macronutrient
        Distribution Ranges (AMDR) suggest adults get roughly 10–35% of calories from protein, 45–65% from
        carbohydrate, and 20–35% from fat — the four presets above sit within or near that range, just shifted
        toward different priorities.
      </p>
      <strong>Source:</strong> Institute of Medicine (2005). <em>Dietary Reference Intakes for Energy,
      Carbohydrate, Fiber, Fat, Fatty Acids, Cholesterol, Protein, and Amino Acids.</em> National Academies
      Press.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/10490/chapter/1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the report →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

